<p class="gna-lede">When a wound on the face is too large to close with stitches alone, after skin cancer removal, an injury, a burn or tumour surgery, it is usually repaired with either a skin graft or a flap. A skin graft is a thin sheet of skin moved without its blood supply, which must pick up new blood vessels from the wound; a flap is tissue moved with its own blood supply still attached. Grafts suit shallow wounds with a healthy base, while flaps are preferred for deeper defects, exposed bone or cartilage, and areas where colour and thickness matching matter most.</p>

<h2 id="short-answer">The short answer</h2>
<p>There is no single "better" technique. The choice depends on the size and depth of the defect, where it is on the face, what is exposed at its base, the quality of the surrounding tissue, and your general health. Often the most suitable plan uses the simplest method that will give a reliable, durable and natural-looking result.</p>
<div class="gna-keypoints">
  <h3>Key points</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>A skin graft needs a healthy, well-supplied wound bed to survive, because it has no blood supply of its own at first.</li>
    <li>A flap carries its own blood supply, so it can cover bone, cartilage or tissue treated with radiotherapy.</li>
    <li>Full-thickness grafts and local flaps usually give the closest match for small facial defects.</li>
    <li>Large or complex defects, such as after oral cancer surgery, may need tissue transferred from elsewhere in the body (a free flap).</li>
    <li>All reconstruction leaves scars; their appearance improves over 12 to 18 months but results vary.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2 id="reconstructive-ladder">The reconstructive ladder</h2>
<p>Surgeons often think about reconstruction as a ladder of options, from the simplest to the most complex:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Healing on its own</strong> (secondary intention), sometimes appropriate for small, shallow wounds in concave areas</li>
  <li><strong>Direct closure</strong> with stitches</li>
  <li><strong>Skin graft</strong>: split-thickness, full-thickness or composite</li>
  <li><strong>Local flap</strong>: neighbouring skin moved into the defect</li>
  <li><strong>Regional flap</strong>: tissue brought from a nearby area, such as the forehead or chest, on a named blood vessel</li>
  <li><strong>Free flap</strong>: tissue moved from a distant part of the body, with its blood vessels reconnected under a microscope</li>
</ol>
<p>The ladder is a way of thinking, not a rule that every step must be tried in turn. Many facial defects clearly need a flap from the start, while others heal well with a graft.</p>

<h2 id="what-is-a-skin-graft">What is a skin graft?</h2>
<p>A skin graft is a piece of skin removed completely from one part of the body (the donor site) and placed on the wound (the recipient site). For the first day or two it survives by absorbing nutrients from the wound bed; new blood vessels then begin to connect over the next few days, and in most cases circulation is re-established within about a week. A pressure dressing, often stitched in place, keeps the graft firmly in contact with the wound during this period.</p>
<h3>Split-thickness skin graft</h3>
<p>This contains the outer layer of skin and part of the deeper layer. It is usually taken from the thigh or upper arm with a special instrument, and the donor site heals like a large graze over one to three weeks, although it can be sore. Split-thickness grafts can cover large areas, but they tend to shrink more as they heal, can look shiny or paler, and often match facial skin less closely.</p>
<h3>Full-thickness skin graft</h3>
<p>This contains the full thickness of the skin. On the face it is typically taken from in front of or behind the ear, the side of the neck, the upper eyelid or above the collarbone, and the donor site is closed with stitches. Full-thickness grafts shrink less and give a better colour and texture match, which makes them useful for the nose, eyelids and ears, but they need a particularly healthy wound bed to survive.</p>
<h3>Composite graft</h3>
<p>A composite graft contains skin together with another tissue, usually cartilage from the ear. It is used for small defects such as the rim of the nostril, where both shape and support need to be restored.</p>
<p>A graft will not usually survive on bare bone without its covering membrane, bare cartilage, tissue that has had radiotherapy, or an infected wound. In those situations a flap is generally needed.</p>

<h2 id="what-is-a-flap">What is a flap?</h2>
<p>A flap is a section of tissue that is moved while keeping its own blood supply, either through a connection to the surrounding skin or through a named artery and vein. Because it does not depend on the wound bed for survival, a flap can cover exposed structures and bring bulk where tissue is missing.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Local flaps</strong> move adjacent skin into the defect by sliding (advancement), pivoting (rotation) or swapping places with nearby tissue (transposition). They give an excellent colour and texture match and can often be done under local anaesthesia.</li>
  <li><strong>Interpolated and regional flaps</strong> bring tissue from a little further away. A classic example is the forehead flap used to rebuild part of the nose, which stays attached to its blood supply for about three weeks before a second operation divides it.</li>
  <li><strong>Free flaps</strong> are taken from elsewhere in the body, such as the forearm, thigh or lower leg, and their vessels are joined to vessels in the neck using microsurgery. A free flap from the lower leg can include bone to rebuild the jaw, and dental implants can sometimes be placed later. Our guide to <a href="/blogs/recovery-protocols-jaw-reconstruction/">recovery after jaw reconstruction</a> explains this in more detail.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="comparison">Skin graft vs flap at a glance</h2>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>&nbsp;</th><th>Skin graft</th><th>Local or regional flap</th><th>Free flap</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Blood supply</td><td>None at first; relies on the wound bed</td><td>Carries its own</td><td>Carries its own, reconnected by microsurgery</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Usually suits</td><td>Shallow defects with a healthy base</td><td>Small to medium defects, exposed cartilage or bone</td><td>Large or complex defects, bone and lining loss</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Colour and thickness match</td><td>Fair to good (full-thickness better)</td><td>Usually very good</td><td>Variable; may need later thinning</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Extra scars</td><td>Donor site elsewhere</td><td>Around the defect, placed along natural lines</td><td>Donor site on the arm, thigh or leg</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Anaesthesia and stay</td><td>Often local or day care</td><td>Local or general; often day care or short stay</td><td>General; several days in hospital with flap monitoring</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Main risks</td><td>Graft loss, contraction, colour mismatch</td><td>Partial flap loss, bulky contour, scar</td><td>Flap failure needing return to theatre, longer recovery</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

