<h2 id="how-surgeons-choose">How the surgeon chooses</h2>
<p>When planning reconstruction, the surgeon considers:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Size and depth</strong>: how much skin, fat, muscle, cartilage, lining or bone is missing</li>
  <li><strong>Location</strong>: the face is divided into aesthetic units, such as the nose, eyelids, lips, cheeks and ears, and scars are hidden along their borders and natural creases where possible</li>
  <li><strong>What is exposed</strong>: bone, cartilage, nerves or the inside of the mouth or nose usually need a flap</li>
  <li><strong>Function</strong>: eyelids must close, lips must seal and nostrils must stay open</li>
  <li><strong>Radiotherapy</strong>: tissue that has been treated, or will be, heals less reliably and usually needs a flap</li>
  <li><strong>Your health</strong>: smoking, diabetes, poor circulation and blood-thinning medicines all affect healing and are planned for</li>
  <li><strong>Timing</strong>: after skin cancer removal, repair is sometimes delayed until the laboratory confirms that the edges are clear</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="common-situations">Common situations where reconstruction is needed</h2>
<h3>Skin cancer on the face</h3>
<p>Basal cell and squamous cell skin cancers are often removed with a margin of normal skin. Small wounds may be closed directly; larger ones on the nose, eyelids or ears are frequently repaired with a full-thickness graft or a local flap.</p>
<h3>Facial injuries</h3>
<p>Road-traffic accidents, falls, assaults and dog bites can cause lacerations with missing tissue. Wounds are cleaned carefully and repaired, often on the same day, and sometimes reconstructed later once swelling and contamination have settled. Learn more about <a href="/facial-injuries">facial injury treatment</a> and how <a href="/blogs/understanding-facial-trauma-management/">facial trauma is managed</a>.</p>
<h3>Oral and head and neck cancer</h3>
<p>Cancers of the mouth, often linked to tobacco, gutka and betel-nut use, may need removal of part of the tongue, cheek lining or jaw. These defects commonly need regional or free flaps to restore speech, swallowing and appearance. Our <a href="/blogs/oral-cancer-surgery-and-reconstruction-guide/">guide to oral cancer surgery and reconstruction</a> explains the process.</p>
<h3>Burns and scar contractures</h3>
<p>Deep burns and tight scars that pull on the eyelids, mouth or neck may be released and reconstructed with grafts or flaps.</p>

<h2 id="recovery-and-risks">Recovery and risks</h2>
<p>After a <strong>skin graft</strong>, the dressing over the graft is usually left in place for about five to seven days. During this time it is important not to rub or knock the area, because movement or blood collecting under the graft are common reasons for it failing. A newly uncovered graft often looks dusky or patchy before it settles. The donor site needs its own care until it heals.</p>
<p>After a <strong>flap</strong>, swelling and bruising are usual for one to two weeks. Stitches on the face are often removed within a week. Flaps can look bulky at first and settle over months, and a minor revision is sometimes offered later. After a free flap, the team checks its colour and blood flow regularly for several days in hospital.</p>
<p>Possible risks of either approach include bleeding, infection, partial or complete loss of the graft or flap, a visible or raised scar, colour mismatch, numbness, and distortion of nearby features such as the eyelid or lip, which may need further surgery. Darker skin is more prone to pigmentation changes and thickened scars. Smoking significantly increases the risk of graft and flap failure and should be stopped well before surgery. Scars mature over 12 to 18 months; sun protection and any massage or silicone treatment your team advises can help, but results vary between individuals.</p>

<h2 id="when-to-see-a-specialist">When to see a specialist</h2>
<p>See a maxillofacial or reconstructive surgeon if you have a facial wound that is not healing, a skin lesion that is growing, bleeding or changing, a scar that is pulling on your eyelid, lip or nostril, or if you have been told you need surgery that will leave a defect on your face. Our <a href="/skin-grafts-and-flaps">skin grafts and flaps page</a> describes the reconstruction we offer.</p>
<div class="gna-callout gna-callout--warn">
  <p><strong>Facial injuries and animal bites need urgent care.</strong> For a deep facial wound, heavy bleeding or a suspected fracture, call 108 or go to the nearest emergency department; our 24/7 facial-trauma line is +91 90006 66476. After a dog or other animal bite, wash the wound with soap and running water for about 15 minutes and seek medical care the same day for rabies vaccination and wound assessment.</p>
</div>
<p>At Gnathos Facial, consultations are held in Gachibowli, Hyderabad, and reconstructive surgery is carried out at NABH-accredited partner hospitals. We explain which options suit your wound, what each involves, and what recovery is likely to look like, so you can decide with full information.</p>
